# A top-level `let` bound to a constrained function with no annotation to fix the instance.
#
# The binding is one global slot holding one closure, so the dictionary chosen at definition is
# the dictionary every use gets — there is no per-use choice to defer to, which is what makes an
# unresolved constraint here an error rather than a polymorphic binding. A `fn` differs precisely
# because it takes its dictionary as a parameter.
#
# Unreported, this compiled to a closure carrying `ToString String`, the guess read off `label`'s
# own return type, and `apply1(labeller, 3)` then read an Int as a string pointer: a SIGSEGV with
# nothing said at compile time. Sibling of ambiguous_class_tyvar — same ambiguity, different site.
module main

fn label(x: a) -> String where ToString a = to_string(x)

fn apply1(f: Int -> String, n: Int) -> String = f(n)

let labeller = label

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} = print(apply1(labeller, 3))
